<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaykh Haydar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1459–<a href="/years/1488">1488</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shaykh Haydar was head of the Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> from 1460 to 1488 and father of Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>. Married to a daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>, he turned the order's followers into a fighting force and is credited with the red headgear that gave them the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>. He was killed in 1488 fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukh-yasar">Farrukh Yasar</a> and his Aq Qoyunlu allies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar.jpg" alt="Shaykh Haydar" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 16th century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheikh_Haydar_Husayni,_and_Sultan_%CA%BFAli_Safavi_(Brother_of_Isma%CA%BFil_I),_detail._Cem%CA%BF-i_T%C4%81r%C4%ABh,_Museum_of_Ethnography,_Ankara,_No._8457,_fol._17a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Haydar was born in June or July 1459 in Amid, present-day Diyarbakır, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-junayd">Shaykh Junayd</a> and Khadija Begum, a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> of the Ak Koyunlu. Less than a year later his father was killed at the Battle of Tabasaran. In 1469–70 Uzun Hasan, who had defeated Jahan Shah of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-koyunlu">Kara Koyunlu</a> at the Battle of Chapakchur, installed Haydar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, and followers of the order from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> came there to join him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Haydar kept the political aims of his father. Under him the Safavid order became a political movement with an increasingly extreme, heterodox Twelver Shi'i colouring, and his followers regarded him as a divine figure. He had them wear a scarlet headgear of twelve gores in memory of the Twelve Imams. He made alliances with leaders of the Talish, Shirvan and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> and led three campaigns into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>, which the historian Roger Savory describes as meant to drill his men against the infidels of Circassia and Dagestan.</p><p>To avoid the lands of the hostile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshahs</a>, Haydar had boats built at Khalkhal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astara">Astara</a> and reached coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. His first seaborne raid plundered the town of Qaytaq and the Hamiri plain, and a first campaign by land followed in 1478. In 1471–1472 he married Halima, also called Alamshah Begum, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-megale-komnene">Despina Khatun</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>; ten sons and four daughters survived him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On his third campaign, in 1488, Haydar sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a>. Outside the Bayqird Castle in Tabasaran he and his men were cornered and, on 9 July 1488, killed by the forces of the Shirvanshah Farrukh Yassar and the Ak Koyunlu Sultan Ya'qub, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>. The Ak Koyunlu had him beheaded and buried the head in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>. He died not far from where his father had fallen.</p><p>His son Ali Mirza succeeded him as head of the order, followed by a younger son who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a>. Ismail later moved his father's remains to the Safavid shrine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, where Haydar's tomb became a place of pilgrimage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shaykh Haydar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruling house was Sunni Muslim, but it showed favour to Sufi orders, and Uzun Hasan married his sister to Shaykh Junayd and his daughter to Junayd’s son <b>Haydar</b>, leaders of the Safavid order of Ardabil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1460 and 1488 Shirvanshah forces killed the Safavid shaykhs Junayd and <b>Haydar</b>, a feud that would destroy the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakır</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Tabasaransky District, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.92,40.22&amp;year=1477" aria-label="Diyarbakır on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5553 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shaykh Haydar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6003 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6003 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.92,40.22&amp;year=1477">What was here in 1477? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
